<article class="env-definition" id="D8A8E5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/D8A8E5">Definition <span data-tag="D8A8E5">8.2.6.1</span> <span class="named">(Smith)</span>.</a>combinatorial model categorymodel category!combinatorial Let $\mathbf{A}$ be model category. We say that $\mathbf{A}$ is <em>combinatorial</em> if the following conditions are satisfied: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The category $\mathbf{A}$ is presentable. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
There exists a set $I$ of <em>generating cofibrations</em>, such that the collection of all cofibrations in $\mathbf{A}$ is the smallest weakly saturated class of morphisms containing $I$ (see Definition <a href="/tag/2E7F80" data-tag="2E7F80">8.1.2.2</a>). 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
There exists a set $J$ of <em>generating trivial cofibrations</em>, such that the collection of all trivial cofibrations in $\mathbf{A}$ is the smallest weakly saturated class of morphisms containing $J$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
